WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.920
My name is Rose George,
and I'm an author and journalist

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.480
and I'm fascinated by blood,
and here's why.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.720
We are stardust and seawater.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.760
The iron in our blood comes from
the death of supernovas -

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.480
like all iron on our planet -

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.520
and it contains salt and water,
like the oceans we came from.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.640
The circulatory system of veins,
arteries, and capillaries

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.320
is about 100,000 kilometres long -
twice the circumference of the Earth.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.280
Most of that is the capillaries -
tiny blood vessels and hollow ways

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.080
that reach nearly every cell
in the body.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.640
You may know that your blood is...

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.280
but there are other types you
might not have heard of -

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:45.200
including

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:49.880
and the epically named...

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:54.200
Animals have blood types too,
dogs have eight,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.680
cats have three.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.840
When humans first tried to transfer
blood between bodies,

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.080
they used the blood
of a lamb and a calf.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.360
Now, we stick to carefully chosen
human blood.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.680
Blood transfusion is so common,
every three seconds worldwide

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.560
someone receives a stranger's blood.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.320
We don't have the same supply
sloshing around our veins

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.240
all our lives.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.440
Our blood is in a constant state
of decay and renewal.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.560
Red blood cells live in our
body for about 143 days.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.280
Decanted into a blood bag,

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.200
they have a shelf life of between
35 and 49 days.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.200
Blood lasts longer than milk,
but not as long as cheese.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.720
In a resting human, the heart pumps a
litre of blood every 10 seconds,

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.680
and it beats on average
75 times a minute.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.800
A blue whale's heart,
the size of a small car,

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.800
beats five times a minute,
and less on deep dives.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.440
A shrew's, a thousand times a minute.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.920
The average human contains
eight to twelve pints of blood,

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.840
but can usually survive
losing up to 40%.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.960
After that point,
the organs begin to shut down

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.320
because they are not being fed
oxygen by blood.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.640
Blood has always been
thought powerful.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.600
The mythical figure of Medusa

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.480
symbolised human
fear and awe of blood.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.920
Blood from the right side
of her body was fatal,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.080
while that from the left side
could restore life.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.640
We may not believe
in Greek myths anymore,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.800
but the force of blood
remains in language.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.960
Blood is essential for human life,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.240
but there is plenty we don't know
about this exceptional substance.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.800
Scientists can now grow
red blood cells in the lab,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.800
but they are extremely expensive. 

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.960
For now, the only way to get this
life-saving, life-enhancing liquid

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.120
is out of the goodness
of someone's heart, 

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.360
and the veins in their arm.

